IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Criminal Appeal No. 1290 of 2019
CITATION: 1992 Supp (1) SCC 335
YEAR: 1992

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: S. Ratnavel Pandian, K. Jayachandra Reddy

PARTIES:
State of Haryana vs Bhajan Lal

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 482 CrPC, Article 226

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
quashing of fir, abuse of judicial process, inherent powers, seven categories

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Formulated the foundational seven illustrative categories governing when High Courts can exercise inherent powers under Section 482 CrPC to quash malicious or legally untenable FIRs.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 482 CrPC, Article 226.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

